      <p class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-[0.2em] uppercase text-accent mb-3">All About Tea</p>
      <h1 class="font-display text-3xl md:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-warm-900 mb-4">{title}</h1>
      <p class="text-xs text-zinc-500 mb-8">Reading time: 9 minutes &middot; Last updated August 2026</p>

      <section id="shen-nung" class="scroll-mt-24 mb-10">
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-warm-900 mb-3">It begins with an accident</h2>
        <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">The founding story of tea is a happy accident. Around 2737 BCE, so the legend goes, the Emperor <strong class="text-warm-900">Shen Nung</strong> (神农), the mythical "Divine Farmer" who taught China to cultivate and taste every plant, was resting in his garden with a cup of hot water. A gust of wind carried leaves from a nearby tree into the cup. The water turned golden and filled the air with fragrance. Curious, the emperor drank — and found it delightful. Tea was discovered.</p>
        <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">The tree was <em>Camellia sinensis</em>, and Shen Nung's cup is where this site's <a href="/types/" class="text-accent underline">six types of Chinese tea</a> all begin. Legend or not, it points at the truth: China is where tea was born, and for thousands of years it was China alone that drank it.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="medicine-to-beverage" class="scroll-mt-24 mb-10">
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-warm-900 mb-3">From medicine to beverage</h2>
        <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">For centuries tea was consumed less as a drink and more as a <strong class="text-warm-900">medicinal remedy</strong> — chewed, or simmered into a bitter broth. Its rise to a beloved beverage came slowly, carried by two forces: Buddhist monks, who drank it to stay alert through long hours of meditation, and the great dynasties that turned it into an art.</p>
        <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">The turning point is the <strong class="text-warm-900">Tang Dynasty (618-907 CE)</strong>. In the Tang, tea cemented itself in China's culture and economy — no longer a medicine, but a social ritual, a luxury good, and a marker of refinement. Tea was pressed into cakes, and preparing one was a craft practiced by <strong class="text-warm-900">hired tea masters</strong>: roast the cake, grind it, whisk it into a bowl. It was an exclusive, expensive ritual.</p>
        <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Over the following dynasties the ceremony kept simplifying. The Song (960-1279) refined the whisked, powdered style. The <strong class="text-warm-900">Ming Dynasty (1368-1644)</strong> swept it aside: whole loose leaves steeped in a bowl — a method so simple anyone could do it, and the direct ancestor of how we brew today. The gaiwan, the <a href="/teaware/" class="text-accent underline">teaware</a> at the heart of modern gongfu, dates from this loose-leaf revolution.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="lu-yu" class="scroll-mt-24 mb-10">
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-warm-900 mb-3">Lu Yu and the Tea Classic</h2>
        <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">China's tea culture found its founding text in <strong class="text-warm-900">Lu Yu</strong> (陆羽), a Tang-era orphan raised in a Buddhist monastery, where he learned to prepare tea for his teachers. He spent years wandering the mountains, gathering wild leaves and studying water — for him, the quality of the water mattered as much as the leaf.</p>
        <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">In 780 CE he published the <em>Cha Jing</em> (茶经, <a href="/tasting-terms/" class="text-accent underline">The Classic of Tea</a>), the world's first complete treatise on tea. It covered cultivation, processing, brewing, water, and teaware, and even catalogued 24 utensils for the ceremony. Today he is honored as the <strong class="text-warm-900">"God of Tea"</strong>, and his insistence on water quality lives on in our <a href="/guides/water/" class="text-accent underline">water guide</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="tea-horse-road" class="scroll-mt-24 mb-10">
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-warm-900 mb-3">The Tea Horse Road</h2>
        <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">By the Tang, tea had outgrown China's borders. The <strong class="text-warm-900">Tea Horse Road</strong> (茶马古道) carried pressed tea — especially <a href="/types/dark/" class="text-accent underline">Yunnan's pu-erh</a> — across the mountains to Tibet, traded for war horses. For over a thousand years the caravans climbed passes where tea, light and valuable, was worth more per pound than almost anything else. The road explains why Chinese tea history is inseparable from <a href="/regions/" class="text-accent underline">Yunnan</a>: it's both the botanical birthplace of the plant and the origin of the trade that spread it.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="gongfu" class="scroll-mt-24 mb-10">
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-warm-900 mb-3">The ceremony: gongfu cha</h2>
        <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">The most refined expression of Chinese tea is <strong class="text-warm-900">gongfu cha</strong> (功夫茶, "brewing with skill"). Born in the tea regions of the south — Fujian and <a href="/types/oolong/" class="text-accent underline">Chaozhou</a> — it uses a high ratio of leaf to water, a small vessel, and many short infusions. The steps are simple and endlessly repeatable:</p>
        <ol class="space-y-2 text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
          <li><strong class="text-warm-800">Warm</strong> the gaiwan and cups with hot water.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-warm-800">Add the leaves</strong> to the warm vessel — the heat releases their scent.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-warm-800">Steep</strong> briefly (15-30 seconds first infusion, adding a few seconds each round).</li>
          <li><strong class="text-warm-800">Pour</strong> through a fairness pitcher so every cup is equal.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-warm-800">Repeat</strong> until the flavors fade — often 5-15 infusions.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">The full method, with temperatures per tea, is in our <a href="/guides/brewing/" class="text-accent underline">brewing guide</a>. Unlike Japan's formal ceremony, gongfu is meant to be lived-in: practiced casually at home, focused on the tea itself rather than strict ritual.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="meditation" class="scroll-mt-24 mb-10">
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-warm-900 mb-3">Tea and meditation</h2>
        <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Tea has been inseparable from Chinese spiritual practice almost since its beginning. Chan (Zen) monks drank it to stay alert in meditation — the tradition captured in the phrase <strong class="text-warm-900">chan cha yi wei</strong> (禅茶一味), "the oneness of Chan and tea." The legend of Bodhidharma even claims tea plants grew where the monk, exhausted from meditation, cast his eyelids to the ground.</p>
        <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Daoism added its own reading. Laozi's teaching of <strong class="text-warm-900">calm and balance</strong> maps neatly onto tea: a simple cup embodies harmony between person and nature, and the mindful preparation of tea becomes a small meditation of its own. The culture section tells more of this story — <a href="/culture/" class="text-accent underline">read it here</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="legends" class="scroll-mt-24 mb-10">
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-warm-900 mb-3">Three legends worth knowing</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 mb-4">
          <div class="card p-5">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-warm-900 mb-1">The Dragon Well</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">A mythic dragon once guarded the mountains above Hangzhou, flying up to gather rain and store it in a well. The tea growing near that well was considered especially precious — which is why the village and its tea are both called Longjing, the <a href="/teas/longjing/" class="text-accent underline">Dragon Well</a>, the king of green teas.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card p-5">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-warm-900 mb-1">The Iron Goddess of Mercy</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">A poor farmer in Fujian tended a neglected temple of Guanyin, the goddess of compassion. In a dream she led him to a sprout in a cave and told him to share it. He planted it, and it grew into a bush with intoxicating, orchid-scented leaves — <a href="/teas/tie-guan-yin/" class="text-accent underline">Tieguanyin</a>, "Iron Goddess of Mercy," named in her honor.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card p-5">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-warm-900 mb-1">The scholar's robe</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">A scholar on Wuyi Mountain, cured of illness by a cliff tea, later draped his red official robe over the bushes in gratitude — giving <a href="/teas/da-hong-pao/" class="text-accent underline">Da Hong Pao</a>, "Big Red Robe," its name. The original mother bushes, over 350 years old, still stand.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="society" class="scroll-mt-24 mb-10">
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-warm-900 mb-3">Tea as society</h2>
        <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Beyond ceremony and legend, tea is China's great <strong class="text-warm-900">social connector</strong>. Serve tea to a guest before the small talk begins — it is the country's most universal gesture of welcome. The <a href="/culture/" class="text-accent underline">wedding tea ceremony</a> marks the bride and groom's transition into partnership. And in the Cantonese south, <strong class="text-warm-900">yum cha</strong> (饮茶, "drink tea") is the weekend institution where tea is poured endlessly alongside dim sum — a shared ritual that has spread around the world.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="modern" class="scroll-mt-24 mb-10">
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-warm-900 mb-3">Tea in modern China</h2>
        <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Today China is the world's largest tea producer, growing all six classic types — the only country that does. And the culture keeps reinventing itself: modern boutique farms, revived <a href="/types/yellow/" class="text-accent underline">rare yellow teas</a>, and a new generation of specialists alongside the ancient customs. The 4,000-year story isn't ending — it's still brewing.</p>
        <div class="card p-5 bg-paper-100">
          <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed"><strong class="text-warm-900">Continue the journey:</strong> start tasting with our <a href="/best-teas/" class="text-accent underline">best Chinese teas</a>, understand the <a href="/guides/production/" class="text-accent underline">production journey</a>, or learn the <a href="/tasting-terms/" class="text-accent underline">tasting vocabulary</a> the masters use.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
